  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Wednesday: While the overall pattern is a ridge\nover top the Pacific Northwest. A weak shortwave will continue \nto pull moisture in form the south. Precipitable water amounts \nwill increase to 0.5-0.6 of an inch or 120-140% of normal. It is\nexpected to only increase cloud amounts as precip chances still\nremain less than 10% through tonight. The ridge briefly \nrebounds behind the shortwave, as a trough starts to dig along \nthe coast on Wednesday. Fog is still expected impact portions of\nthe Inland Northwest but confidence is low on where and how low\nvisibility will be. Highs will be in the upper 40s to low 50s. \nOvernight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s.\n\nThursday through Saturday: The trough will push across the \nregion bringing mainly Cascade precip as the rain shadow effect \nwill limit precip across the rest of the region. With snow \nlevels around 4500 feet, Cascades passes are expected to get \nlight snow. Washington Pass has a 25-75 probability of 1 to 2 \ninches. Stevens is less with a Trace to 1 inch. Sprinkles are \npossible across the rest of the region. Another wave will \nquickly follow with more snow for the passes. Washington has a \n1-3 inch spread. Stevens has a Trace to one inch expected. On \nFriday, winds will be breezy across the Basin with gusts into \nthe low 20 mph range. Highs continue in the upper 40s to low \n50s. Overnight lows stay in the upper 20s and 30s.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: The extended portion of the forecast \nhas trended to a cooler, wetter pattern as the ridge axis will \nstretch from CA to Central MT. It will allow a trough to funnel\na steady stream of warm, moist air into the region. It brings \ndecent snow amounts to Washington Pass with a 25-75 probability\nspread of 1 to 6 inches by Monday morning. Winds will also be \nbreezy across the Basin on Sunday with gusts into the 30s \npossible. The region can expect a weak atmospheric river \ncontinually bringing rounds of precip to the Cascades through \nTuesday. Temperatures trend warmer. Highs will be in the upper \n40s to 50s. Overnight lows stay in the 30s and low 40s. /JDC",
